the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    nonreflexive.
    nonsymmetric.
    nontransitive.
    reflexive.
    symmetric.
    transitive.
    *a node* is a node.
    *a node* is the p of *a second node*.

the knowledge base graphs includes:

the text of the ruleset is at "sources/graphs.rules".

reflexive if
    it is not the case that nonreflexive.

nonreflexive if
    a node is a node
    and it is not the case that the node is the p of the node.

symmetric if
    it is not the case that nonsymmetric.

nonsymmetric if
    a node is the p of a second node
    and it is not the case that the second node is the p of the node.

transitive if
    nontransitive.

nontransitive if
    a node is the p of a second node
    and the second node is the p of a third node
    and it is not the case that the node is the p of the third node.

scenario dataset is, as stated in the dataset:
    a is a node.
    b is a node.
    c is a node.
    d is a node.
    e is a node.
    a is the p of a.
    a is the p of b.
    a is the p of c.
    b is the p of a.
    b is the p of b.
    b is the p of c.
    c is the p of a.
    c is the p of b.
    c is the p of c.
    d is the p of d.
    d is the p of e.
    e is the p of d.
    e is the p of e.
    nonreflexive expects answers [].
    nonsymmetric expects answers [].
    nontransitive expects answers [].
    reflexive expects answers ["reflexive"].
    symmetric expects answers ["symmetric"].
    transitive expects answers [].

query nonreflexive is:
    nonreflexive.

query nonsymmetric is:
    nonsymmetric.

query nontransitive is:
    nontransitive.

query reflexive is:
    reflexive.

query symmetric is:
    symmetric.

query transitive is:
    transitive.

